1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,824
Google just launched a cheaper AI that edges out its own bigger model.

2
00:00:03,874 --> 00:00:07,117
It's called Gemini 4 Hydrogen.

3
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:09,502
Priya writes and searches all day.

4
00:00:09,552 --> 00:00:14,746
Google says Hydrogen runs two and a half times faster than its older Flash model.

5
00:00:14,796 --> 00:00:19,219
Free app users get it, and the API starts at a dollar fifty.

6
00:00:19,269 --> 00:00:23,703
On one independent index, Hydrogen scores fifty-one.

7
00:00:23,753 --> 00:00:26,454
Its bigger sibling, Argon, scores fifty.

8
00:00:26,504 --> 00:00:27,983
That's close to a tie.

9
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:29,758
Best model ever, Muja?

10
00:00:29,808 --> 00:00:30,932
Not quite.

11
00:00:30,982 --> 00:00:33,892
A hydrogen balloon, Muja?

12
00:00:33,942 --> 00:00:40,016
Fun fact: hydrogen is the lightest element, and about three quarters of all normal matter.

13
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:40,992
Okay...

14
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:42,460
back to our story.

15
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:43,595
Now the catch.

16
00:00:43,645 --> 00:00:47,423
On terminal work, Claude Sonnet five point five beats it.

17
00:00:47,473 --> 00:00:51,151
The weights are closed, and most big scores are Google's own.

18
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:52,479
So what should you do?

19
00:00:52,529 --> 00:00:55,236
For everyday work and Search, just use it.

20
00:00:55,286 --> 00:00:58,783
Developers, try it first, and move up when it gets stuck.

21
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:02,178
My take: a strong default, not the new best.

22
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:06,079
Before you build anything big on it, wait for independent tests.

23
00:01:06,129 --> 00:01:08,520
The full review is on my blog.
